{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"standard form of a quadratic equation standard form of a quadratic equation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md","fileName":"Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Quadratic Equations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Quadratic Equations","Quadratic Formula","Zero Product Property","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"standard form of a quadratic equation standard form of a quadratic equation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md","fileName":"Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation","heading":"Standard Form of a Quadratic Equation","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$ax^2+bx+c=0,$$\n\nwith (a\\neq0). Terms are collected on one side in descending powers, and the other side is zero.\n\nStandard form exposes the coefficients used by the [[Quadratic Formula]] and permits factorization to use the [[Zero Product Property]]. An equation such as (8x^2-2x=15) becomes (8x^2-2x-15=0). Distributing, combining like terms, or clearing denominators may be required before the form is visible.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Quadratic Equations","Quadratic Formula","Zero Product Property","a